I was debating whether I should write this set of notes. Almost everyone who understand me well enough asks me why am I learning chemistry? Before I began this course (5.111sc), I disliked chemistry. The subjective reason is due to my poor exposure to chemistry with teachers who would say "just remember it" for exceptional cases of rules, which is rather frequent in chemistry. The objective reason is the lack of generality of chemistry rules, they seem often not fundamental enough.

However, 5.111sc does a great job on quenching my contempt for chemistry by attempting to derive most rules from first principles and going back to them when the rule doesn't work. Also, AI's have gotten good enough to answer my questions (eg. why does silver nitrate stain? can three atoms for a bond?). The professor is also convincing in her argument of the usefulness of a rule that's not the most general and fundamental: "I like simple things that works $90%$ of the time."

This set of notes is based mainly on the class of 5.111sc from MIT OpenCourseWare @drennan2014chemicalscience. Specifically, lectures by Prof. Catherine Drennan, recorded in 2018, and problems from its problem set. When the source of an example problem is not otherwise stated, it may be assumed to be from the problem sets from the course material.

Topics of study in this course, by virtue of MIT Registrar’s Office, is as following. “Introduction to chemistry, with emphasis on basic principles of atomic and molecular electronic structure, thermodynamics, acid-base and redox equilibria, chemical kinetics, and catalysis. Introduction to the chemistry of biological, inorganic, and organic molecules.” @mit_chemistry_fall_2026

Emphasis would be placed on explaining and predicting phenomenons, especially counterintuitive ones, from mathematical and/or physical first principles. This set of notes is, by design, lighter than the mathematical notes, such as Notes on Linear Algebra @saint_even_linear_algebra_notes_2026, as most well-known results, to the extent of those being in the AP curriculum, are skimmed through.


Although officially, this course has no prerequisites, basic chemistry knowledge, math, and physics are assumed to a limited extent.
